  "handoff": {
    "summary": "Defined an immutable O(1) bulk-construction specification for Sage-compatible explicit-density random matrices. The ordinary Python oracle distinguishes ZZ keep-first collisions, QQ and small-GF(p) replacement collisions, and dense-GF(2) inclusive Bernoulli sampling; preserves seeded callback order, density clamping, floor-per-row behavior, and zero-axis edge cases; and hands the complete schedule to exactly one storage-specific constructor without materializing a dense zero matrix or host-side entry list.",
    "risks": [
      "This lane specifies semantics and the one-call boundary but intentionally does not wire public random_matrix or implement domain storage constructors.",
      "Sage's dense GF(2) zero-axis path returns before density coercion, unlike its ZZ, QQ, and small-prime row-draw implementations; integration must retain this observable distinction.",
      "The bulk constructor must own one shared seeded stream and each domain's exact value distribution. Generating positions and values in separate host passes would change reproducibility and collision consumption.",
      "The benchmark is a retained pre-integration scalar-loop baseline; its public timings are not performance gates under concurrent native-suite load."
    ],
    "next_steps": [
      "Register the module and focused test from the integration lane without moving sparse random semantics back into matrix.py.",
      "Add storage-specific bulk constructors for generated FmpzMatrix and FmpqMatrix resources, packed uint64 prime matrices, and M4RI GF(2) matrices.",
      "Have public random_matrix build one domain-appropriate specification, cross one boundary, and publish the completed matrix transactionally.",
      "Add clean-host performance gates against the retained 1000x1000 density=0.1 baseline and Sage after public integration."
    ]
  }
